{"seq": 52, "type": "tool_finished", "ts": "2026-11-22T16:33:58.664896+00:00", "payload": {"call_id": "call_01a2041b76e37f619ca288fe", "is_error": false, "output": "exit code 0\n/opt/maelstrom/doc/04-crdts/01-g-set.md:250:What about network partitions? Can we still make progress when the network is\n/opt/maelstrom/doc/04-crdts/01-g-set.md:251:partitioned? Are writes lost?\n/opt/maelstrom/doc/04-crdts/01-g-set.md:254:$ ./maelstrom test -w g-set --bin g_set.rb --time-limit 30 --rate 10 --nemesis partition\n/opt/maelstrom/doc/04-crdts/01-g-set.md:259:This system delivers *total availability*: even when nodes are partitioned away from the network, reads and writes can still take place. There's no impact on individual request latency!\n/opt/maelstrom/doc/04-crdts/01-g-set.md:261:![A plot of latency over time, showing that operations continue successfully even while a partition is active.](latency-partitions.png)\n/opt/maelstrom/doc/04-crdts/02-counters.md:263:Now we can handle decrements as well! Is it partition-tolerant?\n/opt/maelstrom/doc/04-crdts/02-counters.md:266:$ ./maelstrom test -w pn-counter --bin counter.rb --time-limit 30 --rate 10 --nemesis partition\n/opt/maelstrom/doc/06-raft/02-leader-election.md:626:We have timeouts that *trigger* elections, but if a network partition occurs,\n/opt/maelstrom/doc/06-raft/03-replication.md:404:elections in the face of network partitions by running the test for\n/opt/maelstrom/doc/06-raft/03-replication.md:405:longer, and adding `--nemesis partition`.\n/opt/maelstrom/doc/06-raft/03-replication.md:408:$ ./maelstrom test -w lin-kv --bin raft.rb --time-limit 60 --node-count 3 --concurrency 2n --rate 1 --nemesis partition\n/opt/maelstrom/doc/06-raft/03-replication.md:418:partitions change.\n/opt/maelstrom/doc/06-raft/04-committing.md:7:$ ./maelstrom test -w lin-kv --bin raft.rb --time-limit 60 --node-count 3 --concurrency 10n --rate 100 --nemesis partition --nemesis-interval 3 --test-count 5\n/opt/maelstrom/doc/06-raft/04-committing.md:34:due to network partitions in longer tests), then we have a chance to observe\n/opt/maelstrom/doc/06-raft/04-committing.md:245:partitions: with luck there'll be a leader election, and we can observe multiple\n/opt/maelstrom/doc/06-raft/04-committing.md:249:$ ./maelstrom test -w lin-kv --bin raft.rb --time-limit 30 --node-count 3 --concurrency 2n --rate 1 --nemesis partition --nemesis-interval 10\n/opt/maelstrom/doc/06-raft/04-committing.md:264:Here, n2 was the initial leader, and n0 took over after a network partition.\n/opt/maelstrom/doc/06-raft/04-committing.md:418:$ ./maelstrom test -w lin-kv --bin raft.rb --node-count 3 --concurrency 4n --rate 30 --time-limit 60 --nemesis partition --nemesis-interval 10 --test-count 10\n/opt/maelstrom/doc/06-raft/04-committing.md:422:While partitions are active, we may (or may not!) see temporary disruption to\n/opt/maelstrom/doc/06-raft/04-committing.md:425:![A latency plot showing operations hanging during partitions, then resolving after a few seconds](final.png)\n/opt/maelstrom/doc/03-broadcast/02-performance.md:276:Maelstrom's network can simulate a variety of network partitions: periods where\n/opt/maelstrom/doc/03-broadcast/02-performance.md:278:partitions into our broadcast system.\n/opt/maelstrom/doc/03-broadcast/02-performance.md:281:$ ./maelstrom test -w broadcast --bin broadcast.rb --time-limit 20 --nemesis partition\n/opt/maelstrom/doc/03-broadcast/02-performance.md:454:$ ./maelstrom test -w broadcast --bin broadcast.rb --time-limit 20 --topology tree4 --nemesis partition\n/opt/maelstrom/doc/03-broadcast/02-performance.md:517:$ ./maelstrom test -w broadcast --bin broadcast.rb --time-limit 20 --topology tree4 --nemesis partition\n/opt/maelstrom/doc/03-broadcast/02-performance.md:546:messages to propagate when the network is partitioned. Likewise, we exchange\n/opt/maelstrom/doc/03-broadcast/02-performance.md:548:broadcast to five nodes, because we had to keep retrying while partitions were\n/opt/maelstrom/