it. Internal gossip type must be registered. Client requests count/total registered.\nWhat about rename wrapper perhaps sends native \"add\"/\"read\" rather than renamed? It says translates every message between client and a node. Need test with exact command to observe.\n\nShould we use Go library at all? Could implement raw JSON to control. But library easier. It logs every message to stderr, high volume at rate 100 (2000 ops) plus gossip ~120 messages/sec—fine. It spawns goroutine per message. Potential callback map etc.\n\nNeed include module dependency. `go mod init node`, `go get...` network unavailable but cache. We can manually go mod and go mod tidy perhaps uses cache. Version given future. Need Go 1.27 installed (perhaps future beta) and module dependency. Let's initialize.\n\nBut let's deeply assess if pure gossip can fail exact prompt final equality due totals including never-acked counts that might later be duplicated? G-counter components only one owner, no duplicates. Fine.\n\nThe grader uses `MAELSTROM_RENAME` translating native g-counter protocol. Does rename translate internal gossip fields/types? It has global mappings. Fields \"delta\"->\"visitors\", \"value\"->\"visitors_so_far\"; types listed. If internal gossip includes field \"value\" for map, wrapper may rename it to \"visitors_so_far\", potentially break our parser on both directions consistently? Let's infer wrapper behavior: It translates every message between client and a node, maybe not node-node messages? \"translates every message between a client and a node\". It likely parses process stdin/out and renames bodies, only external side, while node-node messages bypass? Our program uses stdout to network, wrapper may intercept all messages and rename any fields/types globally. If it sees internal gossip body type e.g. \"replicate\", not mapped; field map perhaps \"counters\" no mapping. Avoid field named \"value\" or \"delta\" to prevent field rename. Use field \"components\". Types avoid mapped names. Then safe.\nIf output total_is body includes visitors_so_far already, rename wrapper may map native read_ok type to total_is and native value to visitors_so_far. Wait our program receives renamed total request and is expected to output renamed total_is with visitors_so_far. The wrapper perhaps expects native protocol from program and translates to renamed external, rather than exposing renamed to program. Need carefully interpret.\n\n`maelstrom-rename` stands in for program, starts it, and translates every message between a client and a node. Maelstrom workload uses native add/read. Environment `MAELSTROM_RENAME='{\"fields\": {\"delta\": \"visitors\", \"value\": \"visitors_so_far\"}, \"types\": {\"add\": \"count\", \"add_ok\": \"counted\", \"read\": \"total\", \"read_ok\": \"total_is\"}}'`. There are two possible directions:\n1. Wrapper translates client native messages into renamed messages before passing to our program, and translates our renamed response back to native. Then program uses count/visitors, total, counted, total_is/visitors_so_far.\n2. Wrapper translates our program's native messages into renamed messages to client, while client native remains native? \"translates every message between a client and a node\" and type maps add->count. If client sends native add, wrapper likely transforms to count before program. Program should speak renamed. The prompt explicitly describes clients send node `count` with `visitors`, responses `counted`, etc. This strongly indicates option 1: wrapper acts protocol adapter on node-facing side. They say \"Maelstrom itself uses other names for these messages. maelstrom-rename stands in for your program, starts it, and translates every message between a client and a node, so test with command above as it is.\" So yes our program should use renamed protocol, wrapper translates.\n\nNeed test exact. Could create basic program log input and run command short. But we'll implement and observe logs. Use `count`, `total` response fields.\n\nWhat does wrapper do with node-node messages? If program sends to another node, likely wrapper intercepts all stdin/stdout and applies renaming to every message, regardless source/dest, despite wording. It might translate types mapped. Our custom unaffected. Fields custom unaffected. Good.\n\nPotential issue internal gossip messages contain msg_id? If no msg_id, Node library identifies as request (in_reply_to zero) and dispatch. Fine. Don't include msg_id to avoid callbacks. Use type \"counter_gossip\".\nPotential issue Node library's `Reply` uses original src and request msg_id. Great.\nPotential issue rename wrapper might add/rename msg_id? Fields map doesn't.\nPotential issue client sends total and expects total_is; return exactly.\n\nLet's inspect `maelstrom-rename` behavior by decompiling Go binary perhaps source path `/tmp/rename/main.go` and symbols. Could use `go tool objdump`, strings enough. Maybe binary built with source embedded? `go version -m`.", "text": "", "tool_calls": [{"arguments": {"command": "go version -m /usr/local/bin/maelstrom-rename; go tool nm /usr/local/bin/maelstrom-rename | grep 'main\\.' | head -100", "timeout_seconds": 120}, "arguments_error": null, "id": "call_01a2041be1c870b3a98c2b95", "name": "shell"}, {"arguments": {"command": "strings /usr/local/bin/maelstrom-rename | grep -E '^main\\.|main\\.' | head -100", "timeout_seconds": 120}, "arguments_error": null, "id": "call_01a2041be26e7193ab047f8d", "name": "shell"}, {"arguments": {"command": "go tool objdump -s 'main\\..*' /usr/local/bin/maelstrom-rename 2>/dev/null | head -300", "timeout_seconds": 120}, "arguments_error": null, "id": "call_01a2041be31570c1ac95f337", "name": "shell"}], "usage": {"input_tokens": 64485, "output_tokens": 1603}}}
